  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "n1",
      "kind": "sound",
      "title": "What a final letter sounds like",
      "text": "The last letter of a syllable is said in a smaller set of sounds than it has when it starts a word. ม, น and ง stay m, n and ng (นม, อัน, สอง). But กบ ends in p, วัด and บาท end in t, and ปาก ends in k: ก, บ and ด are said k, p and t at the end, and so are the letters that sound like them. Read the end of each word as one of these six sounds and the spelling stops being a surprise.",
      "explains": [
        "th.practice.finals"
      ],
      "afterScenes": 1
    }
  ],
